title: Relics Heresy – Vigilantius vs. Jerome
description: The debate around 406 AD between Vigilantius and Jerome over relic veneration, night vigils and praying to saints, with Vigilantius holding to Scripture against creeping pagan customs.
output: lessons/history/relics-vigilantius-jerome.html

# From the notes (studies/data/notes/relics-vigilantius-jerome-notes.html), in the author's words, arranged on slides.

=== Introduction
--- Relics Heresy [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: Relics Heresy
sub: Vigilantius vs. Jerome
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides.

--- Corpse worship
lead: The abomination of corpse worship is a pagan, eastern practice that has no support in scripture. It is a heretical tradition originating with vipers.

=== The debate
--- An early flashpoint
tile: c. 370–after 406 AD | **Vigilantius**
tile: c. 347–420 AD | **Jerome**
p: The debate between Vigilantius (c. 370–after 406 AD) and Jerome (c. 347–420 AD) is one of the clearest early flashpoints over creeping paganism and carnal traditions like relic veneration, ascetic practices, and other emerging customs in the church...

--- The surviving source
step: 406 AD | Jerome’s *Against Vigilantius* was a defense of the practices (written in 406 AD in a single night) and is the main surviving source of this debate.
step: Lost | Vigilantius’s own writings are lost (likely destroyed by the Roman Church).

--- The presbyter from Gaul
p: Jerome, who had hosted Vigilantius in Bethlehem, wrote in reaction to an epistle by the presbyter from Gaul, which criticized the growing trends of
- veneration of relics, the bones and ashes of martyrs,
- night vigils at gravesites that had pagan cultish overtones,
- as well as the growing gnostic asceticism, doctrines of devils, demanding celibacy, dietary prohibition,
- praying to and invoking the saints’ intercession,
- and the collection of alms by churches to send to the church at Jerusalem, which was growing in wealth while local poor were neglected.

--- Making merchandise
callout: Similar to modern debates defending these then-new practices as “traditions,” it came down to biblical fundamentalism being opposed by people with a financial stake in making merchandise of gullible people.

=== Ashmongers
--- Ashmongers
lead: Vigilantius called relic-venerators “ashmongers” (*cinerarii*) and idolaters for worshiping “a bit of powder wrapped up in a costly cloth,” seeing it as pagan-like under a Christian cloak.
p: Saints’ souls are with God in heaven and have no special ongoing link to their earthly remains.

--- Jerome’s response
lead: Jerome’s response is exactly the sort of rationalizing cope we are used to today.
- He said it was “honoring, not worship,” similar to the “veneration!” semantics.
- He claimed supernatural occurrences were reported at the death vigils.
- That Vigilantius was promoting sexual immorality.

=== Close
--- A later development [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: A later development
center: This event shows that the tradition that the Catholic Church and Orthodox claim is from the Apostles was a later development, and the labeling of Vigilantius as a heretic for opposing these new customs, destroying his writing was how the emerging political entity dealt with critics.
